Maybe your phone was moved while you were away. Maybe you saw a "Try again in 30 seconds" message you didn't cause. Or maybe you just want to know if someone has been trying to get into your phone.

Here's the short answer:

- **Android doesn't keep a record** of failed unlock attempts, and it doesn't take a photo of the person who made them.
- **You can add this with an app** that takes a picture when someone enters the wrong PIN, pattern or password. That photo is often called an *intruder selfie*.
- **CrookCatcher** does exactly this, for free, and emails you the photo together with the location.

## 🔍 Can Android show who tried to unlock your phone?

Not by default. When someone enters the wrong PIN, pattern or password, Android simply rejects it. After several wrong attempts it makes the person wait before trying again, but it doesn't save a log you can check later, and it never uses the camera.

Some phone manufacturers include a similar "intruder photo" option in their own app lock. These usually only protect individual apps, not the lock screen itself, so they won't catch someone trying to get into your phone in the first place.

To see who tried to unlock your phone, you need an app that watches the lock screen and reacts to failed attempts.

## 📸 How to take a photo of anyone who enters the wrong password

This is how to set it up with CrookCatcher. It takes a few minutes, and you don't need to create an account.

1. **Install CrookCatcher** from <a href='https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.harteg.crookcatcher&referrer=utm_source=website&utm_medium=blog_who_tried_unlock_steps' target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Play</a>.
2. **Allow Device Administrator.** This is the Android permission that lets an app notice when an unlock attempt fails. CrookCatcher uses it only for that.
3. **Allow camera and location access**, so it can take the photo and record where it happened.
4. **Connect a Gmail account** for the alert emails. CrookCatcher can only *send* emails from it; it can't read, delete or change anything in your inbox. You can create a separate Gmail account just for this, or skip email entirely and only keep the photos on your phone.
5. **Test it.** Lock your phone, enter a wrong PIN on purpose, then unlock it. You should see your own (probably confused) face in the app and in your inbox. 😄

That's it. From now on, every time someone enters the wrong PIN, pattern or password, CrookCatcher silently does its job in the background.

## 📬 What you get when someone tries to unlock your phone

Each time an unlock attempt fails, you get:

- **A photo from the front camera** of whoever was holding the phone
- **The GPS location** and an estimated address of where it happened
- **The time** of the attempt
- **An email alert** with all of the above, so you have it even if the phone is gone

All photos are also saved in the app, where you can browse every attempt with its time and location.

With **CrookCatcher Pro**, you can also record a video with sound, take a photo of the surroundings with the back camera, upload everything to Google Drive automatically, and catch people even when they *guess your PIN correctly* after a failed attempt (break-in detection).

## 🧐 Other signs someone has been using your phone

A photo is the clearest proof, but these can also tell you someone has been on your phone:

- **Recently opened apps** you didn't use (open the app switcher)
- **Messages or notifications marked as read** that you haven't seen
- **Google account activity** you don't recognize, at <a href="https://myactivity.google.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">myactivity.google.com</a>
- **The phone is in a different place** or position than you left it

If you notice any of these, setting up an intruder selfie app is the easiest way to find out who it is next time.

## 🚨 What to do if it was a thief

If the photos show a stranger, or your phone is missing:

1. **Don't confront the person yourself.** Your safety matters more than the phone.
2. **Report it to the police** and include the photos, time and location from the alert email.
3. **Use Google's Find Hub** to locate, lock or erase the phone.
4. **Contact your carrier** to block the SIM and the phone's IMEI.

To make your phone harder to steal in the first place, read our guide to the [top 5 Android settings to secure your phone against theft](/blog/top_five_settings).

## ⚖️ Only use it on your own phone

CrookCatcher is made to protect *your* phone. Only install it on a device you own. Secretly monitoring someone else's phone isn't what it's for, and in many countries it's illegal.

